-/**
- * A redirect operand's RAW text is nothing but literal characters and plain variable references —
- * `$NAME`, `${NAME}` — with no command substitution anywhere in it.
- *
- * FOUND BY INDEPENDENT REVIEW of the 2026-09-18 redirect exemption, and it falsified that
- * exemption's own rationale. `echo hi > \`rm -rf ../<other lane>/src\`` was ALLOWED on the branch and
- * `ask` on main. The mechanism is entirely in this file's lexer: the backtick is not in `OPERATORS`,
- * so `lex` folds the substitution into ordinary words, `segments` takes the single word after `>` as
- * the redirect target (`` `rm ``) and scatters the rest into the `echo` segment, whose verb mutates
- * nothing. `ops` came back empty and the only unresolved entry was a `redirection` standing in the
- * session's own claim — exactly the shape the exemption was written to spare. The `$( … )` spelling
- * of the identical command was DENIED, because `(` and `)` are operators and `CHAIN_OPS` boundaries,
- * so the inner `rm` re-segments and zones as a sibling. Same semantics, opposite answers, and the
- * ALLOWED one ran `rm -rf` on another lane.
- *
- * WHY THIS IS A WHITELIST AND NOT THE OBVIOUS BLACKLIST. The review's prescription was to reject a
- * raw containing a backtick or `$(`, borrowing `globBounded`'s `[^$\`]`. Measured against this
- * lexer, that is not sufficient: for `> $(rm -rf x)` and `> $((1+1))` the `(` is an OPERATOR, so it
- * never reaches the operand token at all and the raw handed to this test is the single character
- * `$`. A substring search for `$(` finds nothing there and spares it. Listing the ways an operand
- * can hide a command means keeping that list ahead of the lexer forever — the same losing shape as
- * an allowlist of cmdlets, argued against at `segments`. So the test is turned around: an operand is
- * spared only if every `$` in it introduces an ordinary variable name and no backtick appears. `$1`,
- * `$?`, `${X:-…}` and a bare trailing `$` are all refused, which is conservative and costs only the
- * sparing: the operand defers to the native permission flow (an `ask` from this hook, when that
- * was written).
- *
- * The lesson is the general one: the exemption rested on a syntactic category ("it is only a
- * redirect") that the parser did not actually enforce, and "a redirect cannot recurse or delete a
- * tree" is true of the operator but not of a command hiding in its operand. So the operand must be
- * positively shown to be a plain name, and everything else goes back to being unspared — then an
- * `ask`, now a deferral to the native permission flow — where it was before, and where the `$( … )`
- * spelling never left.
- *
- * IT IS TWO LINEAR PASSES AND NOT ONE REGEX, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
- *
- * The first cut of this test was a single alternation:
- *
- *   /^(?:[^$`]|\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*|\$\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\})*$/
- *
- * which reads correctly and BACKTRACKS EXPONENTIALLY. `[^$\`]` and the tail of
- * `\$[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` both match `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, so every `$`-run has as many parses as its
- * length and the parses multiply across runs; on an input that does NOT match, the engine explores
- * all of them before giving up. Measured on the regex alone: 5 runs 0.3ms, 6 runs 2.2ms, 7 runs
- * 19ms, 8 runs 137ms — about 7x per added run.
- *
- * Driven against the real hook process, which `.claude/settings.json` gives `"timeout": 10`, an
- * 82-character operand — nine `$aaaaaaaa` runs followed by a backtick — ran the full 10 seconds and
- * was KILLED, emitting nothing. A hook that emits nothing has no opinion, so the call falls through
- * to the normal permission flow; `origin/main` answers `ask` for that same command. A crafted
- * operand therefore deleted this guard's opinion and burned a core for ten seconds, and it needed no
- * `cd` at all, because this test runs before `cwdZone` and at least twice per operand. The
- * vulnerability did not exist on main — it arrived with the exemption, which makes it ours.
- *
- * So the ambiguity is removed rather than tuned. JS has no atomic groups and no possessive
- * quantifiers, so the single ambiguous alternation becomes two unambiguous passes: reject a backtick
- * outright, then delete every well-formed `$NAME`/`${NAME}` and see whether any `$` survives. The
- * replace pattern has no ambiguity to backtrack into — both alternatives are anchored at `$` and are
- * disjoint on the character after it — so the whole check is linear in the length of the operand.
- * The semantics are unchanged; `shell-ownership-assert.test.mjs` pins the full refuse/spare table
- * against this predicate and puts a wall-clock bound on it, so re-introducing the alternation turns
- * a test red instead of quietly turning the guard off.
- */
-export const isPlainRedirectName = (raw) =>
-  !raw.includes("`") &&
-  !raw.replace(/\$(?:[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*|\{[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\})/g, "").includes("$");
